# transport — every connection hactl opens takes its bound from the caller's
# --timeout, never from a constant alone.
#
# The surface is derived by internal/surfaceaudit.TransportSurface from the
# typed source: every composite literal of http.Client, http.Transport,
# net.Dialer or websocket.Dialer, plus every request issued through
# http.DefaultClient, which has no timeout at all. A site is one such decision,
# keyed by file, enclosing function and type — one function legitimately makes
# several, and each is a separate bound. See dev/surfaces/README.md.
#
# Why it is a census and not a violation surface: there is no syntactic test for
# "this bound came from the flag" that survives one level of indirection, and the
# indirection is the fix — the two HTTP callers now share haapi.HTTPClient rather
# than each constructing their own. What the gate can guarantee is that the set
# is closed: a fourth transport anywhere in the tree is unclassified on the day
# it appears, and somebody has to write down what bounds it.
#
# The state this replaces: three transports, three files, three changes, and the
# flag reaching two of them. `companion status --timeout 1s` against a host that
# never answers returned after 10.02s — 1s, 3s and 20s all landing on the same
# number — while `health --timeout 1s` against the identical host aborted at
# 1.01s (#73). Nothing was findable from the outside: the flag was documented and
# most of the product obeyed it.
#ceiling 0

internal/haapi/transport.go:HTTPClient:http.Client = proven: TestHTTPClientIsBoundedByTheCallerTimeout
internal/haapi/transport.go:HTTPClient:http.Transport = proven: TestHTTPClientIsBoundedByTheCallerTimeout
internal/haapi/transport.go:HTTPClient:net.Dialer = proven: TestHTTPClientIsBoundedByTheCallerTimeout
internal/haapi/websocket.go:(*WSClient).connect:websocket.Dialer = proven: TestWSConnectHonoursTheCallerTimeout
internal/haapi/websocket.go:(*WSClient).connect:net.Dialer = proven: TestWSConnectHonoursTheCallerTimeout
